\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Massive graded Wilson data}
\date{2026-11-09}

\p{The \newvocab{massive graded Wilson data} consist of the following operators and measures. Let #{d\geq1}, let each period #{N_j\geq1} be an integer, and put #{\Lambda=\prod_{j=1}^d\mathbb Z/N_j\mathbb Z}. The lattice spacing is #{a>0}; #{x\in\Lambda} is an integer site label, with physical position #{ax}. All fields and links are periodic. Equip the finite field space #{\mathcal V=\ell^2(\Lambda)\otimes S} with its counting inner product. The spin space #{S} is a nonzero complex Hermitian space of declared even dimension #{n_s}, with supplied self-adjoint Clifford endomorphisms and grading
##{\begin{gathered}
\gamma_j^*=\gamma_j,\qquad \gamma_i\gamma_j+\gamma_j\gamma_i=2\delta_{ij}I_S,\\
\Gamma^*=\Gamma,\qquad\Gamma^2=I_S,\qquad\Gamma\gamma_j\Gamma=-\gamma_j\quad(1\leq j\leq d).
\end{gathered}}}

\p{The grading acts at each site and is part of the input in every lattice dimension. The integer #{n_s} records the supplied spin multiplicity. Every Grassmann generator associated with either grading eigenspace is odd; the involution #{\Gamma} does not specify Grassmann parity.}

\p{Choose a nonzero integer charge #{q} and the representation #{z\mapsto z^q} of #{U(1)}. The positive links are labeled by #{(x,j)}, with tail #{x} and head #{x+e_j}, and carry independent values #{U_j(x)\in U(1)}. For #{N_j=1} these links are loops; for #{N_j=2} the two positive links between a pair of adjacent sites remain distinct bonds. Reverse traversal of a labeled link uses its inverse, rather than the value on another positive bond with opposite endpoints. Define
##{\begin{aligned}
(T_j^U\psi)(x)&=U_j(x)^{-q}\psi(x+e_j),\\
((T_j^U)^{-1}\psi)(x)&=U_j(x-e_j)^q\psi(x-e_j),\\
D_{a,U}&=\frac1{2a}\sum_j\gamma_j(T_j^U-(T_j^U)^{-1}),\\
L_{a,U}&=-\frac1{a^2}\sum_j(2I-T_j^U-(T_j^U)^{-1}),\\
K_U&=mI+D_{a,U}-c_WL_{a,U},\qquad m>0,\quad c_W\geq0.
\end{aligned}}}

\p{Thus the usual Wilson parameter #{r\geq0} corresponds to #{c_W=ra/2}; the factor #{a} is retained because #{L_{a,U}} contains #{a^{-2}}. These formulas extend the \vocabk{Wilson operator}{fdip-009U} to the declared supplied spin module and all positive periods, including the zero Wilson coefficient. Put #{n=\dim_{\mathbb C}\mathcal V=n_s|\Lambda|}. The determinant of an endomorphism means its scalar action on the top exterior power.}

\p{For #{g:\Lambda\to U(1)} the active gauge action is #{U_j^g(x)=g_{x+e_j}U_j(x)g_x^{-1}} and #{(G_gv)(x)=g_x^qv(x)}. This is the \vocabk{source-to-head convention}{fdip-006D}. Let #{d\lambda(U)} be normalized product Haar measure on all positive links. Choose a finite nonnegative Borel gauge-invariant action #{S_g(U)}. Its weight #{w_g=e^{-S_g}} is strictly positive and at most one. For the \vocabk{plaquette holonomy}{fdip-006N}, the canonical action is
##{S_g(U)=\beta\sum_{x\in\Lambda}\sum_{j<k}(1-\operatorname{Re}U_{jk}(x)),\qquad\beta\geq0,}}

\p{where
##{U_{jk}(x)=U_k(x)^{-1}U_j(x+e_k)^{-1}U_k(x+e_j)U_j(x).}}

\p{The plaquette action uses the unit-charge \vocabk{connection}{fdip-006D} itself; the matter transport uses its declared power #{q}. A real number that is not an integer does not define this representation of #{U(1)}.}
